1
00:00:00,014 --> 00:00:03,524
Speaker: If you asked Burt Reynolds,
near the end of his life, to name his

2
00:00:03,563 --> 00:00:08,474
biggest regret, it was not a movie he
turned down or a stunt that went wrong.

3
00:00:09,264 --> 00:00:13,754
It was one woman and one relationship he
ended more than three decades earlier.

4
00:00:14,563 --> 00:00:18,303
In twenty fifteen, at the peak
of writing his own life story,

5
00:00:18,873 --> 00:00:23,223
Reynolds sat down to publish a
memoir called But Enough About Me.

6
00:00:24,143 --> 00:00:28,023
He could have filled it with car
chases, cowboy hats, and the biggest

7
00:00:28,033 --> 00:00:29,773
box office years of his career.

8
00:00:30,693 --> 00:00:35,744
Instead, he gave an entire chapter to
a woman he had not spoken to in years.

9
00:00:36,363 --> 00:00:38,463
He called her the love of his life.

10
00:00:39,003 --> 00:00:43,214
He called her the one who got away,
and he kept saying it in interview

11
00:00:43,214 --> 00:00:47,134
after interview until the phrase
followed him into his own obituaries.

12
00:00:47,823 --> 00:00:52,993
Here is the question worth asking: not
whether Burt Reynolds loved Sally Field.

13
00:00:53,694 --> 00:00:59,203
He said that part out loud, in print, on
camera for decades, and nobody who watched

14
00:00:59,203 --> 00:01:01,053
him say it ever doubted he meant it.

15
00:01:01,753 --> 00:01:05,664
The real question is why the woman he
was describing kept saying, in her own

16
00:01:05,673 --> 00:01:10,313
words, that the man in his memory was not
quite the man she remembered living with.

17
00:01:11,323 --> 00:01:14,304
The story starts on a film set in 1976.

18
00:01:15,094 --> 00:01:18,414
Reynolds was already one of the biggest
box office draws in the country.

19
00:01:19,273 --> 00:01:23,033
Field was known mostly for television,
for the girl-next-door parts that

20
00:01:23,033 --> 00:01:26,113
had followed her since she was a
teenager, and she was hungry to

21
00:01:26,124 --> 00:01:27,913
be taken seriously as an actress.

22
00:01:28,783 --> 00:01:32,484
They were cast opposite each other in
a car chase comedy nobody expected to

23
00:01:32,493 --> 00:01:37,996
become a phenomenon, a movie called
Smokey and the Bandit They had known

24
00:01:37,996 --> 00:01:41,326
each other only a handful of days
before the chemistry became obvious

25
00:01:41,346 --> 00:01:43,106
to everyone standing near the camera.

26
00:01:44,046 --> 00:01:48,056
Reynolds later said it felt less like
four days and more like four years.

27
00:01:48,926 --> 00:01:54,316
Field, in her own telling, felt the
same electricity, the same sense that

28
00:01:54,316 --> 00:01:57,806
something had clicked into place faster
than either of them could explain.

29
00:01:58,826 --> 00:02:01,966
The movie became one of the
highest-grossing films of the year,

30
00:02:02,496 --> 00:02:06,906
beaten at the box office only by a
new space adventure called Star Wars.

31
00:02:07,755 --> 00:02:11,596
Off-camera, the two leads became
one of Hollywood's most-watched

32
00:02:11,625 --> 00:02:16,455
couples, photographed constantly,
asked about constantly, and for a

33
00:02:16,455 --> 00:02:20,176
while, the public romance looked
exactly like the on-screen one.

34
00:02:20,986 --> 00:02:22,965
Magazine covers wanted them together.

35
00:02:23,515 --> 00:02:25,485
Talk shows wanted them together.

36
00:02:26,375 --> 00:02:30,605
Reynolds was, at that point, arguably
the single biggest movie star working

37
00:02:30,655 --> 00:02:35,846
anywhere in the world, and the fact that
he had chosen his co-star in real life

38
00:02:36,166 --> 00:02:40,015
over any of the other women he could
have been photographed with only made

39
00:02:40,015 --> 00:02:43,665
the story more irresistible to the public
that was watching from the outside.

40
00:02:44,715 --> 00:02:49,526
Over the next five years, they made
three more films together: Hooper, The

41
00:02:49,526 --> 00:02:51,986
End, and Smokey and the Bandit Two.

42
00:02:52,865 --> 00:02:56,295
On screen, the pairing
worked every single time.

43
00:02:57,145 --> 00:03:00,665
Audiences kept buying the romance
because for a while it was real.

44
00:03:01,575 --> 00:03:04,396
Reynolds proposed marriage more
than once during those years.

45
00:03:05,005 --> 00:03:08,805
Field turned him down every time and
later said she always sensed his heart

46
00:03:08,825 --> 00:03:12,775
was not fully behind the offer, that
some part of him wanted the idea of

47
00:03:12,776 --> 00:03:14,765
a wife more than the reality of one.

48
00:03:15,505 --> 00:03:19,455
That detail matters because it is the
first sign that the two of them were

49
00:03:19,456 --> 00:03:23,675
not living inside the same relationship,
even while they were living inside the

50
00:03:23,675 --> 00:03:29,035
same house, sharing the same films, and
being photographed by the same cameras.

51
00:03:30,126 --> 00:03:33,936
For a few years, the cracks
stayed almost entirely private.

52
00:03:34,706 --> 00:03:37,096
Then came the movie that
ended things for good.

53
00:03:38,036 --> 00:03:43,396
In 1979, Field was offered the lead
role in a small drama called Norma

54
00:03:43,396 --> 00:03:47,796
Rae, about a textile worker who
organizes her factory into a union.

55
00:03:48,616 --> 00:03:52,726
It was nothing like the glossy comedies
that had made her famous, and nothing

56
00:03:52,726 --> 00:03:54,525
like anything Reynolds wanted for her.

57
00:03:55,575 --> 00:03:59,225
According to Field's own account,
he objected to the character, made a

58
00:03:59,225 --> 00:04:02,975
demeaning comment about the woman's
past, and threw the script back at

59
00:04:02,975 --> 00:04:04,725
her rather than simply saying no.

60
00:04:05,685 --> 00:04:09,716
When she pushed back and said she wanted
the part anyway, he told her she was

61
00:04:09,716 --> 00:04:13,405
letting the whole thing get the better
of her, as though wanting to stretch as

62
00:04:13,405 --> 00:04:15,585
an actress was some kind of overreaction.

63
00:04:16,425 --> 00:04:17,985
Field took the role anyway.

64
00:04:18,716 --> 00:04:23,505
She met with the director, signed on, and
spent weeks on a factory floor playing

65
00:04:23,505 --> 00:04:27,286
a woman with nothing in common with the
parts that had made her rich and famous.

66
00:04:28,155 --> 00:04:31,705
Reynolds, by her account, then tried
to keep her from attending the film's

67
00:04:31,755 --> 00:04:37,215
premiere at the Cannes Film Festival, the
kind of moment that could define a career.

68
00:04:38,015 --> 00:04:39,185
She went regardless.

69
00:04:39,715 --> 00:04:44,626
Norma Rae won her the first of two Academy
Awards for Best Actress, and it happened

70
00:04:44,626 --> 00:04:48,675
despite the man she was living with,
not because of any support from him.

71
00:04:49,565 --> 00:04:54,145
That was the real turning point,
more than the award ceremony itself.

72
00:04:55,075 --> 00:04:58,855
For years, Field had been introduced
as the woman in Burt Reynolds'

73
00:04:58,855 --> 00:05:03,206
movies, and by extension, the
woman in Burt Reynolds' life.

74
00:05:04,085 --> 00:05:08,485
Norma Rae was the first time she stepped
fully into her own identity as an actress,

75
00:05:08,596 --> 00:05:11,276
on her own terms, against his objections.

76
00:05:11,965 --> 00:05:15,096
Field later said that experience
marked the beginning of her pulling

77
00:05:15,096 --> 00:05:16,955
away from the relationship for good.

78
00:05:17,565 --> 00:05:22,055
They stayed together off and on for a
little while longer, drifting rather

79
00:05:22,056 --> 00:05:25,935
than deciding, but the ending was already
written the moment she walked onto

80
00:05:25,935 --> 00:05:27,865
that factory set without his blessing.

81
00:05:28,835 --> 00:05:32,345
In 1982, they split for the final time.
